<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/005M">Example <span data-tag="005M">1.5.2.4</span> <span class="named">(Non-Commuting Squares)</span>.</a> Let $K$ denote the boundary of the product $\Delta ^1 \times \Delta ^1$: that is, the simplicial subset of $\Delta ^1 \times \Delta ^1$ given by the union of the simplicial subsets $\operatorname{\partial \Delta }^1 \times \Delta ^1$ and $\Delta ^1 \times \operatorname{\partial \Delta }^1$. Then $K_{\bullet }$ is a $1$-dimensional simplicial set, corresponding to a directed graph which we can depict as </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =40pt@C=40pt{ \bullet \ar [r] \ar [d] &amp;  \bullet \ar [d] \\ \bullet \ar [r] &amp;  \bullet .} \]
</div>
<p> We can then display a $K$-indexed diagram in an $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ pictorially </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =40pt@C=40pt{ C_{00} \ar [r]^-{f} \ar [d]^{g} &amp;  C_{01} \ar [d]^{g'} \\ C_{10} \ar [r]^-{ f'} &amp;  C_{11}, } \]
</div>
<p> where each $C_{ij}$ is an object of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, $f$ is a morphism in $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ from $C_{00}$ to $C_{01}$, $g$ is a morphism in $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ from $C_{00}$ to $C_{10}$, $f'$ is a morphism in $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ from $C_{10}$ to $C_{11}$, and $g'$ is a morphism in $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ from $C_{01}$ to $C_{11}$. </p>
</article>
